/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a mass m (kg) on a spring of stiffness k (N/m) with a viscous damper c (N·s/m), displaced by x(t) (m) and driven by a force F(t).
   - ωn is the undamped natural frequency (rad/s); fn the same in Hz; ζ the damping ratio (dimensionless); cc = 2√(km) the critical damping (N·s/m).
   - Damping sets the character of free motion. ζ < 1 is underdamped: the mass oscillates at ωd = ωn√(1 − ζ²) while the motion dies away. ζ = 1 is critically damped: the fastest return with no overshoot. ζ > 1 is overdamped: a slow return with n
   - Under a harmonic force F₀ sin Ωt the steady response has amplitude X, which depends on the frequency ratio r = Ω/ωn:
   - X is the amplitude (m); F₀/k the static deflection under F₀ (m); r = Ω/ωn.
   - Near r = 1 the response peaks: resonance.
   - A real wing has distributed mass and stiffness, so it has many natural frequencies, each with a mode shape in which all points move in step: first bending, first torsion, second bending and so on.
   - M is the generalised mass (kg); K the generalised stiffness (N/m); μ the mass per unit length (kg/m); EI the bending stiffness (N·m²); φ″ the second derivative of the assumed shape; the integrals run over the span.
   - This is Rayleigh's method.
   - Divergence is static.
*/
