/* book Worked example 2: Q: A wing is modelled as a uniform cantilever 6 m long with EI = 5 × 10⁶ N·m² and μ = 50 kg/m. Estimate its first bending frequency by Rayleigh's method with φ = 1 − cos(πx/2L), and compare with the exact 3.516√(EI/μL⁴). Then a wing section with K_θ = 200 kN·m/rad, e = 0.15 m, S = 4 m² and a₁ = 5.7 per rad flies at sea level (ρ = 1.225 kg/m³). Find its divergence speed.
   1. The shape has φ = 0 and φ′ = 0 at the root x = 0, as a cantilever needs. φ″ = (π/2L)² cos(πx/2L).
   2. K = EI (π/2L)⁴ ∫₀ᴸ cos²(πx/2L) dx = EI (π/2L)⁴ L/2 = (π⁴/32) EI/L³ = 3.044 EI/L³.
   3. M = μ ∫₀ᴸ [1 − cos(πx/2L)]² dx = μL(3/2 − 4/π) = 0.2268 μL.
   4. ω = √(K/M) = √(3.044/0.2268) × √(EI/μL⁴) = 3.664 × √(5 × 10⁶/(50 × 1296)) = 3.664 × 8.784 = 32.2 rad/s (5.12 Hz).
   5. Exact: 3.516 × 8.784 = 30.9 rad/s (4.92 Hz). Rayleigh is 4.2% high, as it must be.
   6. Divergence: qD = K_θ/(e S a₁) = 200,000/(0.15 × 4 × 5.7) = 58,480 N/m².
   7. VD = √(2 × 58,480/1.225) = 309 m/s. At half this dynamic pressure the twist from any initial incidence is already doubled.
   Answer: ω ≈ 32.2 rad/s by Rayleigh (30.9 rad/s exact); VD = 309 m/s. */
