/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A shear force per unit length Nxy (N/m) on the edges of a plate gives a shear stress τ = Nxy/t.
   - Nxy,cr is the critical shear force per unit length (N/m); τcr the critical shear stress (Pa); ks the shear buckling coefficient; D the plate stiffness (N·m); b the shorter side of the panel (m).
   - a is the longer side (m).
   - Clamped edges raise ks; it is read from charts for that case and must not be mixed with the simply supported fit.
   - When several loads act, express each as a ratio to the stress that would buckle the panel under that load alone: Rc = σ/σcr for compression and Rs = τ/τcr for shear.
   - Rc is the compressive stress ratio σ/σcr and Rs the shear stress ratio τ/τcr, each with its single-load critical stress.
   - The shear ratio is squared because shear of either sign is equally bad: reversing the shear only mirrors the inclined buckle, so the curve must be symmetric in Rs.
   - If all loads grow in proportion by a factor λ, buckling happens when λRc + λ²Rs² = 1.
   - λ is the factor on the applied loads at buckling; MS the margin of safety, positive when the panel does not buckle.
   - Other combinations, such as bending with shear in a spar web, or biaxial compression, have their own interaction curves with different exponents, taken from design charts.
*/
